  author={Nadeem, Numair and Asad, Muhammad Hamza and Ullah, Muhib and Bais, Abdul},
  journal={IEEE Transactions on AgriFood Electronics}, 
  title={Geometry-Guided Plant Stand Count Estimation in Densely Populated Lentil Fields}, 
  year={2026},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={1-17},
  abstract={The agriculture industry faces increasing pressure to enhance food production while ensuring sustainability. One critical aspect of this challenge is to achieve consistency in crop establishment, which ensures optimal resource utilization and uniform crop maturity, ultimately increasing crop yield and reducing chemical inputs to the field. Traditional methods of quantifying the consistency of crop establishment, such as manual plant counting, are labor-intensive, error-prone, and inefficient for large-scale applications. Deep learning-based solutions are developed to automate these methods. However, their applicability is primarily limited to sparsely populated crops where individual plants are visually separable. In densely populated crops, such as lentils, minimal interplant spacing and rapid canopy closure cause seedlings to merge into blobs, where each blob contains multiple plants, and individual plant boundaries disappear. This article presents a novel pipeline to analyze the consistency of crop establishment for densely populated crops. Our proposed approach extracts crop blobs by segmenting crop pixels using a domain-robust semantic segmentation model based on a vision transformer. In parallel, convolutional neural network combined with image processing techniques is applied to identify crop rows, including their orientation and a row-width buffer, thereby enabling the assignment of blobs to their corresponding rows (on-row blobs). Geometric and contextual features are then extracted from the blobs, and a regressor predicts the number of plants within each on-row blob. Finally, statistical postprocessing integrates per-blob counts to quantify plant stand counts per-row and per-image for consistency of crop establishment analysis. The experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of the method in predicting plant stand count to ensure consistency of crop establishment.},
  keywords={MIMICs;Millimeter wave integrated circuits;Monolithic integrated circuits;Pixel;Radio frequency;Radio access networks;Regional area networks;Digital images;Electronic mail;Radio communication;Crop and weed detection;crop consistency;plant count estimation;plant density;probabilistic Hough transform (PHT);row detection;semantic segmentation;smart agriculture},
